{"text": " We're seeing grown, profitable companies bolting old aircraft engines to the ground because they're desperate for power. Two developers reportedly paid $25 million, not for a turbine, just to reserve a place in line for one. It's the machine behind roughly 40% of America's electricity, and they're among the most complex pieces of machinery on Earth. Only a handful of companies can actually build one. Three manufacturers control over three-quarters of the global market. One of them is GE Vernova. Its order backlog has ballooned from roughly 80 gigawatts to 116, and its own CEO says the company will be largely sold out through the end of 2028. And in 2024 alone, developers ordered nearly three times more turbine capacity than every factory on Earth could physically produce in a single year. Which means it doesn't matter how many zeros Big Tech puts on the check, the manufacturers are structurally constrained. But it's not just the gas turbines either. Transformers which are the machines that step power down from the grid now take over 2.5 years to get with an estimated 30% supply shortage. And for new transmission lines, they take 6.5 years on average to complete due to permitting delays, backlogs, and a mountain of other BS. So it's not just a single waitlist that's backed up, it's the entire American energy supply chain. We're seeing tech move at Netflix speeds and the infrastructure underneath it moving at the speed of Blockbuster.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 5.82, "text": " We're seeing grown, profitable companies bolting old aircraft engines to the ground because they're desperate for power.", "tokens": [50363, 775, 821, 4379, 7334, 11, 17967, 2706, 11572, 889, 1468, 6215, 11874, 284, 262, 2323, 780, 484, 821, 12111, 329, 1176, 13, 50663], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15556480771019346, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 0.003818939672783017, "words": [{"word": " We're", "start": 0.0, "end":